virglrenderer: clip distances from a vertex shader straight to an evaluation shader as varyings on the Mac (#589)

What: on a desktop GL without cull distances (the Mac's 4.1) a vertex shader that writes
clip distances and is followed by a tessellation evaluation shader with no control shader
passes them as plain varyings, omacvm_clip0 (and omacvm_clip1 for more than 4), without a
location, instead of gl_ClipDistance in its gl_PerVertex block. It says so in its variable
info (tess_clip_out, num_out_clip = the distances it stores); the evaluation shader's key
gets tess_clip_in from the vertex shader when there is no control shader, and the
evaluation shader then reads omacvm_clipK[v][i] as virgl-shader-tess-clip-varyings.patch
made it do. No gl_ClipDistance is left in such a vertex shader. Not as the last stage, not
with a control shader, not with cull distances, nor when the varyings would not fit
(MAX_VARYING): then both keep gl_PerVertex as before. GLES and hosts with cull distances
keep their GLSL.
Why: Apple's GL on Metal builds no pipeline for such a draw either ("Fallback to SW vertex
processing because buildPipelineState failed"): on the M1 Max (macOS 27) it ran on Apple's
CPU vertex path, drew nothing, took 250-261 ms for the first draw, and Metal's shader
compiler crashed for the separable shape. The same values as plain varyings build on the
GPU and draw the right pixels. Apple's software renderer has no such failure.
Upstream: not sent (a workaround for Apple's GL).
Test: Tests/virgl/test-core-glsl-shaders.c: the GLSL of a vertex shader with clip distances
before an evaluation shader without a control shader on the Mac's config (whole and
separable, 1 and 8 distances) has no gl_ClipDistance and compiles on the Mac's GL; with a
control shader, as the last stage, with cull distances or on GLES it keeps its text.
Tests/virgl/test-whole-program-shapes.c C17, C18, C21 and C22 draw the same (the only test
of the key change in vrend_renderer.c).

